{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation","id":1298612,"title":"Climate of Creation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-03-22T09:19:18-07:00","id":1298612,"title":"Climate of Creation","content":"Something I've always kinda had a difficulty with is figuring out what exactly the climate of each region is like in Creation, so I was wondering if anyone out there has made something like a climate map, or has some solid guidelines.<br /> <br /> My kinda gut reaction is the southern border regions being roughly equivalent to the equator and the northern border being polar, but that still leaves a lot of room to figure it temperate, subtropical, etc areas","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:17:16-07:00","id":1298641,"title":"","content":"Each pole exemplifies it's element so the closer you get to that pole the more of <i>that</i> you get. It's hotter in the South closer to the pole of Fire. It's colder in the North, closer to the pole of Air. It ranges north to south between that. West and East have more to do with content that temperature: more water in the West and more Forests to the East. So coastal areas are likely to be more temperate and just get more extreme as you go north or south.<br /> <br /> The exemption is the Blessed Isle which is built as &quot;Creation in miniature&quot; in 3e. In past editions it was &quot;temperate everywhere&quot; but now you'll get hot tropics on the south coast and colder climbs to the north. Swamps to the west and good farmland and woods to the east.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I post Artifacts in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>. How I make them is in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/828820-how-to-write-artifacts-and-evocations\" target=\"_blank\">this thread</a>.<br /> I have made many tools and other things for 3rd Edition. I now host all of my creations on my Google site: <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/unsungherovault/home\" target=\"_blank\">The Vault of the Unsung Hero</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/368-the-unsung-hero","name":"The Unsung Hero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2288"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:32:39-07:00","id":1298648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Unsung Hero</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298641#post1298641\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Each pole exemplifies it's element so the closer you get to that pole the more of <i>that</i> you get. It's hotter in the South closer to the pole of Fire. It's colder in the North, closer to the pole of Air. It ranges north to south between that. West and East have more to do with content that temperature: more water in the West and more Forests to the East. So coastal areas are likely to be more temperate and just get more extreme as you go north or south.<br /> <br /> The exemption is the Blessed Isle which is built as &amp;quot;Creation in miniature&amp;quot; in 3e. In past editions it was &amp;quot;temperate everywhere&amp;quot; but now you'll get hot tropics on the south coast and colder climbs to the north. Swamps to the west and good farmland and woods to the east.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm perfectly comfortable with the broad strokes of the poles and such, where I trip up is more on the local scale. Take The scavenger lands, for example. Both Nexus and Thorns are in the same &quot;region&quot;, so do they have the same general climate? Or is it more like comparing Minnesota to Texas?<br /> <br /> Alternatively, where exactly does &quot;typically temperate but with longer, really nasty winters&quot; end and &quot;vast expanses of frozen tundra&quot; begin when going north?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:43:51-07:00","id":1298653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SamuraiMujuru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation#post1298612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My kinda gut reaction is the southern border regions being roughly equivalent to the equator</div> </div> </div> </div>Nah, way hotter than that.<br /> <br /> Earth's climate is weird. The hottest countries in the world aren't on the equator like you'd assume. The hottest city in the world is Kuwait city. The hottest average temperature of a country is Sudan. Places like Equatorial Guinea, Indonesia, Kenya, Colombia, Brazil... yeah, they're very hot, but nowhere near the worst.<br /> <br /> I had a friend who lived in Oman, which is pretty far from the equator. If he wanted to visit his neighbour, he'd drive. For like a minute. Because driving out of his garage and around to his neighbour's garage in an air-conditioned car was bearable, while walking for 1-2 minutes to his neighbour's door would be unbearable.<br /> <br /> Whereas when I was in Kenya, yeah, it was really hot... but not, like, totally unbearable. Just hot. I actually remember the day I stood on the equator, it was probably about 30c?<br /> <br /> Winters where I live now reach a low of about, what, -1c? Whereas I used to live in a city far south of my hometown, where winters reached <i>-37c</i>. When celsius and farhenheit are the same, you know it's too cold. I used to get frozen eyelashes, frozen beard, frozen eyebrows... That'll never happen in the UK, but it's much further north here.<br /> Also, while the winter was bitterly cold, it was pretty dry. A dry winter is just mind-blowing for a Brit. Here, it's very simple; summer is (relatively) hot and dry, winter is cold and wet. Yet there are other places in the world where those don't go together.<br /> <br /> <br /> Creation's climate is more simple (though I guess you'll still get issues like more extreme climates further from the sea, its colder in the mountains, etc).<br /> <br /> The further north you go, the colder it is.<br /> The further south you go, the hotter it is.<br /> <br /> The slight oddity is the south is dry, and the west is wet, not the north. So the wettest parts of the world and the dryest aren't opposite each other. So, say, the north-west is brutally cold, but it's probably fairly average in terms of wet/dry.<br /> <br /> (Presumably the Primordials did this because fertile land needs a balance of hot and dry)<br /> <br /> But still, I think generally it changes gradually.<br /> <br /> <br /> So basically you end up with:<br /> North - Cold and windy<br /> North-West - Cold and Wet<br /> West - Wet<br /> South-West - Hot, weather varies between a dry season and a monsoon season<br /> South - Hot and Dry<br /> South-East - Hot<br /> East - Temperate, lots of plants<br /> North-East - Cold<br /> Centre - Temperate, no extreme weather, great soil<br /> <br /> And that's partly why the Centre and East have the most fertile land and are the best places to live.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:46:36-07:00","id":1298654,"title":"","content":"Climate in Creation is definitely going to be a bit trickier than on Earth. For one thing, there's effectively two heat sources - the Sun emits heat and all that, as it does on Earth, but also, the Elemental Pole of Fire pushes heat northwards as it ascends in the cycle of the poles. Another complicating factor is the Sun's course through the sky does actually move over the course of the year - it's further north in summer, and further south in winter. <br /> <br /> I think the combination of these factors is going to mean that the North has colder winters but warmer summers than equivalent areas on Earth, while the South will generally have <i>very</i> hot summers, but milder winters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:48:04-07:00","id":1298655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SamuraiMujuru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298648#post1298648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm perfectly comfortable with the broad strokes of the poles and such, where I trip up is more on the local scale. Take The scavenger lands, for example. Both Nexus and Thorns are in the same &quot;region&quot;, so do they have the same general climate? Or is it more like comparing Minnesota to Texas?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Minnesota's much further north than Texas.<br /> <br /> Nexus and Thorns are about the same.<br /> <br /> Nexus should be more woody. Of course, they've chopped it all down, but maybe there's some woods nearby. It's probably not a very noticeable difference though.<br /> Thorns is by the coast, so probably has more moderate weather.<br /> <br /> I shouldn't think they will be extremely different though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Alternatively, where exactly does &quot;typically temperate but with longer, really nasty winters&quot; end </div> </div> </div>The southern coast of the White Sea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and &quot;vast expanses of frozen tundra&quot; begin when going north? </div> </div> </div>A bit north of the northern coast of the White Sea.<br /> <br /> If you look at the map, I think you can essentially tell from the way it's coloured.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\"> http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:50:45-07:00","id":1298658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298654#post1298654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another complicating factor is the Sun's course through the sky does actually move over the course of the year - it's further north in summer, and further south in winter.</div> </div> </div> </div>Does it? I've always wondered, but never got an answer. Does it say that somewhere in the books?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think the combination of these factors is going to mean that the North has colder winters but warmer summers than equivalent areas on Earth, while the South will generally have <i>very</i> hot summers, but milder winters. </div> </div> </div>I don't see why the North would have more extreme weather than places like Russia and Canada do in real life.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T10:51:53-07:00","id":1298659,"title":"","content":"Incidentally, this is a good thread. It's the kind of world-building stuff people generally forget about. And it makes a change from the normal charm comparison or which Exalt type is best. Good work SamuraiMujuru.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T11:18:14-07:00","id":1298676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298655#post1298655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Minnesota's much further north than Texas.<br /> <br /> Nexus and Thorns are about the same.<br /> <br /> Nexus should be more woody. Of course, they've chopped it all down, but maybe there's some woods nearby. It's probably not a very noticeable difference though.<br /> Thorns is by the coast, so probably has more moderate weather.<br /> <br /> I shouldn't think they will be extremely different though.<br /> <br /> <br /> The southern coast of the White Sea.<br /> <br /> <br /> A bit north of the northern coast of the White Sea.<br /> <br /> If you look at the map, I think you can essentially tell from the way it's coloured.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/\" target=\"_blank\"> http://armrha.org/exaltedmap/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div></div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, the map helps, but especially in this edition it's more, I dunno, thematic than literal, and it also doesn't address things like seasons and such (icehome has proper summers if memory serves, but is solid white on the map. Rubylak is right across from where the white really begins, but isn't described as being overly cold.)<br /> <br /> Of course, you do have specific areas that futz with things (like Iscomay and its Oma Valley) but I'm looking for broader than cities/countries but more specific than region/pole.<br /> <br /> Though Thorns isn't as far south as I was thinking it was (it's more Minnesota to Missouri. Which is still a noticeable difference, but less extreme.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":882,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","user":"SamuraiMujuru","date":". 03-22-2019, 02:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T11:27:51-07:00","id":1298679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298659#post1298659\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Incidentally, this is a good thread. It's the kind of world-building stuff people generally forget about. And it makes a change from the normal charm comparison or which Exalt type is best. Good work SamuraiMujuru.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks! It's something I didn't used to think about until a mix of listening to game craft podcasts and having a game that is heavily centered around a guild caravan, so crawling across creation in ways climate and weather changes are more gradual but also more relevant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T11:47:54-07:00","id":1298690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298658#post1298658\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does it? I've always wondered, but never got an answer. Does it say that somewhere in the books?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not in the books, no, but I'm pretty sure the devs said, at some point in the old Ask the Devs thread, that they thought the Sun moved north and south over the course of the year, as part of the general policy of &quot;Creation more or less looks like Earth except as noted&quot;. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see why the North would have more extreme weather than places like Russia and Canada do in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, as I said, the North is kind of getting a double-whammy of cold during the winter - the Pole of Fire is at its lowest ebb at that point, and the Pole of Air is ascendant, and the Sun itself is further south as well, so its rays are less intense. <br /> <br /> Of course, mitigating against this is the fact that nowhere in the North is really as continental (that is, far from large bodies of water) as either Canada or Russia is. The place furthest from the sea is probably someplace on the north edge of the Wasting Tundra, which is about 1500 miles north of the White Sea. Compare that to the interior of Canada, which can get about 2500 miles from the sea, or Russia, which can be even further. So the most continental areas of Creation might be less so than Earth. On the other hand, the places in Creation most distant from water are also closest to their respective Poles, as well, which is going to change the equation again. Like I said, it's complicated!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T12:03:07-07:00","id":1298693,"title":"","content":"Creation's basically like the part of northern hemisphere focused on North African and Western Eurasia*, writ large (like, <a href=\"http://66.media.tumblr.com/ffa151f7829e5ec5c2c04bba0df0c1b4/tumblr_mmvne81oOQ1rhvgmao1_1280.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">see</a>), with far less change in climate over the same distance. Albeit, the extremes of cold and heat at the poles are more intense, and the topology is lots more rugged in many places, many microclimates, etc. <br /> <br /> *3e's map is actually even more like this than 1e and 2e I think, since the greater extent of the north and south matches more closely North African and the Arctic Circle, while Dreaming Sea somewhat parallels the Indian Ocean (both thematically and in shape).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T12:25:47-07:00","id":1298699,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298693#post1298693\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Creation's basically like the part of northern hemisphere focused on North African and Western Eurasia*, writ large (like, <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;http://66.media.tumblr.com/ffa151f7829e5ec5c2c04bba0df0c1b4/tumblr_mmvne81oOQ1rhvgmao1_1280.jpg&amp;quot;\" target=\"_blank\">see</a>), with far less change in climate over the same distance. Albeit, the extremes of cold and heat at the poles are more intense, and the topology is lots more rugged in many places, many microclimates, etc. <br /> <br /> *3e's map is actually even more like this than 1e and 2e I think, since the greater extent of the north and south matches more closely North African and the Arctic Circle, while Dreaming Sea somewhat parallels the Indian Ocean (both thematically and in shape).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's pretty much exactly how I've been figuring it (except worded far more suck in my original post.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><b><i>Exalted Behind a Screen of Jade, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar</i>, Good Sitting Dog, Best Lurking Cat with Bones, Pioneer Pooch, Corsair, <i>Director, Keeper of the Karstein Manor</i>, Scion with Shield of Knowledge, Erymanthian Boar,<i> Seeking Awakened, Cloaked Changeling, Family Head, Kindred, Agent, Poltergeist</i>, Disciple of the Antler Crown, Wraith, Conspiracy Theorist, <i>First Time Traveler</i></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/882-samuraimujuru","name":"SamuraiMujuru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar882_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1129"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T12:28:01-07:00","id":1298701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298690#post1298690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not in the books, no, but I'm pretty sure the devs said, at some point in the old Ask the Devs thread, that they thought the Sun moved north and south over the course of the year, as part of the general policy of &quot;Creation more or less looks like Earth except as noted&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I always thought it was a sensible thing to say, but I don't remember them ever actually saying so. I remember asking and mostly the question was avoided. But I could be remembering wrong.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, as I said, the North is kind of getting a double-whammy of cold during the winter - the Pole of Fire is at its lowest ebb at that point, and the Pole of Air is ascendant, and the Sun itself is further south as well, so its rays are less intense. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but why hotter summers?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, mitigating against this is the fact that nowhere in the North is really as continental (that is, far from large bodies of water) as either Canada or Russia is. The place furthest from the sea is probably someplace on the north edge of the Wasting Tundra, which is about 1500 miles north of the White Sea. Compare that to the interior of Canada, which can get about 2500 miles from the sea, or Russia, which can be even further. So the most continental areas of Creation might be less so than Earth. On the other hand, the places in Creation most distant from water are also closest to their respective Poles, as well, which is going to change the equation again. Like I said, it's complicated! </div> </div> </div>Sort of.<br /> It's complicated to compare to Earth, but I think it's actually simpler in terms of straightforward &quot;how hot is place X.&quot; It's just that Earth's climate is really complicated because we change position, distance from the sun, have this weird band thing going on, and that's not even getting into our magnetic field.<br /> I think Creation is simpler. A wizard did it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T14:29:57-07:00","id":1298748,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298701#post1298701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, but why hotter summers?</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, the same logic - during the summer in the North, the sun is making it hotter via the same mechanism as on Earth, its rays being more intense since it's further north, but also the Pole of Fire is pushing heat northwards since it's in its ascending period. <br /> <br /> I actually like that difference compared to Earth, because I think it makes large populations more plausible in the northen regions - the winters may be colder, but they're not <i>longer</i>, meaning a society that could grow/collect enough food to live through an Earth winter should be able to do much the same in Creation, while the warmer summer means that growing more food overall and a wider variety of food is plausible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T15:29:35-07:00","id":1298764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Basically, the same logic - during the summer in the North, the sun is making it hotter via the same mechanism as on Earth, its rays being more intense since it's further north, but also the Pole of Fire is pushing heat northwards since it's in its ascending period. </div> </div> </div>True, but I think it's probably best to assume the Pole of Fire and Sun, together, end up producing about the same amount of heat as on Earth. It gets confusing otherwise.<br /> <br /> Eh, Britain's pretty far north and has a big population. As do Germany and European-Russia. Belgium and the Netherlands have colossal population density, and are both quite far north.<br /> <br /> I don't really see any value in saying the North has hotter summers than equivalents in the real world. It'll just confuse players and STs to give, say, Karn much higher average summer temperatures than Denmark, or Diamond Hearth much higher temperatures than Reykjavik.<br /> <br /> If you want the North to have much bigger populations than medieval Europe+Canada+Central Asia (the North is a big place), I'd suggest magic. Greenfield Sites, geomancy (like in the Omo valley), first age fertility magic, etc.<br /> <br /> I'll point out that in 2nd ed, the population of Creation was hinted to be about 100 million per direction on average, with the North being smaller than some of the others. So let's say about 70 million... which is about the same as Europe in the Middle Ages. (And the North is much, much bigger than Europe.)<br /> <br /> So I don't really think it really needs any explanation for big populations, because it's population isn't really all that big.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 03-22-2019, 07:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T16:45:55-07:00","id":1298782,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298764#post1298764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh, Britain's pretty far north and has a big population. As do Germany and European-Russia. Belgium and the Netherlands have colossal population density, and are both quite far north.</div> </div> </div> </div>These are all examples of places with mild winters compared to Canada. <br /> <br /> Germany hardly ever sees snow and I'd be surprised that they've ever seen -50 C temperatures that we got in central Canada this winter. <br /> <br /> This is thanks in large part to the ocean climate and the warm ocean current flowing past. <br /> <br /> Latitude is not nearly as important as where your air is coming from in regards to climate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T17:09:22-07:00","id":1298786,"title":"","content":"That's a point I was making earlier; I lived in a city way south of Britain, with -37 winters.<br /> <br /> That city... had 8 million people. But to be fair, historically it wasn't so dense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 03-22-2019, 08:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T17:27:02-07:00","id":1298794,"title":"","content":"The 1e book Savage Seas has some info on navigation and, iirc, indicates that the Sun moves over the course of the year.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T17:31:25-07:00","id":1298796,"title":"","content":"So how about this for climate in creation: <br /> <br /> The sun suppresses the pole it's closest to. During the summer months it passes closer to the north which reduces the effect of the pole of air and allows the pole of fire to thrive. During the winter months the opposite is true and the pole of fire is lessened while the pole of air runs rampant. Obviously this doesn't take in to account the other three poles but it's a start?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T18:04:24-07:00","id":1298804,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298653#post1298653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Centre - Temperate, no extreme weather, great soil<br /> <br /> And that's partly why the Centre and East have the most fertile land and are the best places to live.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Blessed Isle is different in this Edition, serving as a microcosm of Creation as a whole; its southern quadrant has hot deserts and even some nomadic inhabitants, its northern quadrant experiences harsh winters and harder soil, its west is swampy and broken up into lots of islets. It's only its eastern portion that is not uniformly fertile and with pleasant weather.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-22T19:03:08-07:00","id":1298816,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298701#post1298701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always thought it was a sensible thing to say, but I don't remember them ever actually saying so.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Creation more or less looks like Earth except as noted&quot; is a good summation of my approach to the material.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T00:19:49-07:00","id":1298872,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298796#post1298796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So how about this for climate in creation: <br /> <br /> The sun suppresses the pole it's closest to. During the summer months it passes closer to the north which reduces the effect of the pole of air and allows the pole of fire to thrive. During the winter months the opposite is true and the pole of fire is lessened while the pole of air runs rampant. Obviously this doesn't take in to account the other three poles but it's a start?</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm, I think that might work, yeah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T00:21:53-07:00","id":1298874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298804#post1298804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Blessed Isle is different in this Edition, serving as a microcosm of Creation as a whole; its southern quadrant has hot deserts and even some nomadic inhabitants, its northern quadrant experiences harsh winters and harder soil, its west is swampy and broken up into lots of islets. It's only its eastern portion that is not uniformly fertile and with pleasant weather.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but they're still a lot less extreme than the far south, north, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T02:02:19-07:00","id":1298881,"title":"","content":"Ive always had Creation be the Mediterranean writ large.<br /> <br /> The Blessed Isle was the equivalent of the Italian Penninsula. Good climate, with variances accounting for regional and elevation (the Blessed Isle is rather mountainous, much like Italy)<br /> <br /> The Southern coast around the Inner Sea was the North African analogue. The further South you went, the more Sahara-y it got.<br /> <br /> The Northern coast around the Inner sea was the equivalent of the Mediterranean coast of France, or the warmer coasts of the Black Sea. The interior was more akin to continental France and Germany, with the southern shores of the White Sea being the Northern and Baltic coastlines.<br /> <br /> The islands off the Northern penninsula between the White and Inner Seas became my Great Britain. <br /> <br /> The Wasting Tundra became Scandanavia, Iceland and as you went further north, Greenland. <br /> <br /> Melas Fangs were the Baltic equivalent, with the Finnish-expies all around.<br /> <br /> The East was the Danube analogue mixed with the Yellow River (the Chinese one). The climate of my Creation could be roughly divided in half by the Yellow River: subtropical to and hotter to the south and temperate and colder to the north. Since the Yellow River is so fucking huge (it is more an inlet of the Inner Sea than a pure river, and is saltwater until Nexus and brackish to Great Forks in my games), it moderates the climate of a lot of the East that would otherwise be *much* more continental.<br /> <br /> My Hundred Kingdoms were really humid in summer, got a lot of rain in spring and autumn, and got a lot of snow in winter, but that was more due to geography than anything else. Only the southernmost realms in the Hundred Kingdoms could grow rice.<br /> <br /> That big ol' grassland to the north of the Yellow River, called the Thunder Plains or the Sea of Grass, was both the Eurasian Steppe and the American Great Plains.VERY continental in climate.<br /> <br /> I never did anything with the West, so i cant talk about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T02:21:29-07:00","id":1298882,"title":"","content":"In terms of North, Centre and South, that's pretty much how I think of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T04:45:18-07:00","id":1298913,"title":"","content":"So I'm getting that you all go with Summer and Winter for Creation's seasons. What happens to the calendar of the five elemental seasons? I know some people incorporate it, fire becomes summer, air becomes winter, maybe some surrounding months get absorbed depending on your region (i.e. water &amp; earth are part of winter in the north &amp; summer in the south), and wood is probably analagous to Spring.<br /> <br /> I dunno though, I personally like to dispense with earthly seasons altogether. But even that involves some improvisation and mental gymnastics (Mask of <i>What</i>?) with all the references to Summer &amp; Winter that have slipped into various sourcebooks.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24421-katamari_naneke","name":"Katamari_Naneke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T05:37:49-07:00","id":1298925,"title":"","content":"I think they did explain that in 3e.<br /> <br /> Here's a calendar...<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://ibb.co/FJbtLB7\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"https://i.ibb.co/929jkqY/Calendar.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></a><br /> <br /> Note that 3rd ed moved Earth to after Summer, as the old 2e calendar had like a 3 week autumn and a massive spring.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T06:34:28-07:00","id":1298934,"title":"","content":"Yeah, they are not called seasons in Ex3. The year is divided into five <b>Cycles</b>, of Air, Water, Wood, Fire, and Earth, each divided into Ascending, Resplendent, and Descending. The four seasons are fitted loosely into the fifteen months calendar (each month four weeks long) because...heavenly beaurocrazy, I guess.<br /> <br /> &quot;I mean, like <i>sure</i>, we <i>could</i> divide those sixty non-calibration weeks into twelve five-week-long months, which would neatly fit three months to each season, but, <i>but</i>, then we'd have to fire three of the calendar gods, and who wants to deal with the fallout of <i>that</i>? No, thanks, we'll stick to fifteen by four-week Lunar Months.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":487,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","user":"Greyman","date":". 03-23-2019, 09:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T07:11:13-07:00","id":1298943,"title":"","content":"I like to imagine it was different in the High First Age, with Solars negotiating with the calendar gods to have a shorter winter, etc, to improve fertility and generally make life better, but after the Usurpation things have returned to a more natural, and less optimal for humans, system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T07:25:17-07:00","id":1298949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Katamari_Naneke</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298913#post1298913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I dunno though, I personally like to dispense with earthly seasons altogether. But even that involves some improvisation and mental gymnastics (Mask of <i>What</i>?) with all the references to Summer &amp; Winter that have slipped into various sourcebooks.</div> </div> </div> </div>And were prominently placed throughout the first Exalted book, such as in the Caste descriptions. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> There was a time when the calendar of the Realm appeared to just be another calendar, rather than an objective description of the nature of Creation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T10:06:21-07:00","id":1298977,"title":"","content":"The elemental cycles and the seasons are both described in Third Edition. They're mentioned in the corebook on Page 73. They're not exactly the same things so I don't think people would use them interchangeably, rather they're just two elements of the calendar. And like in real life, there are probably places where the seasons don't break down in quite the same way and people use terms like rainy season versus dry season, monsoon season, etcetera. There's also probably places that don't use this calendar, but you know, it's a game, so for the sake of ease, here's one you can use for your game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Violet Green, Freelancer</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23657-violetdreamer","name":"VioletDreamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23657_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"163"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T11:04:03-07:00","id":1298992,"title":"","content":"I'll also point out that there's a real world difference between 'midwinter' on the calendar and when you're half way through winter. <br /> <br /> Solstice and equinox dates are not very well mapped to the true seasons or temperature, though this is probably more evident in some locations than others","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my homebrew exalt: The Fabulists - Chosen of the Raksha <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1141861-peach-new-exalt-splat-the-fabulist-exalts-chosen-of-the-raksha\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8804-armyofwhispers","name":"armyofwhispers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8804_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T17:03:29-07:00","id":1299079,"title":"","content":"I can't remember where I got this from, so it's probably not canon, but I have this image of the calendar coming about through a meeting between a delegate of the Solar Deliberative and the Bureau of Seasons that went something like this.<br /> <br /> Solar delegate: This is the model that the Deliberative has approved. It is orderly and symmetrical. Can you implement it?<br /> <br /> Bureau functionary: Well... Yes we can do it, but in order to make it so, there'll need to be a five-day period of Calibration between the years. It'll be a time of darkness, when the weather will be wild and ungoverned, the moon and stars will disappear from the sky, the dead will trouble the living, dread spirits will enter Creation and vile curses will fall upon any project that people work on.<br /> <br /> Solar delegate: Hm? I stopped listening after you said you could do it.<br /> <br /> Bureau functionary: ...<br /> <br /> Solar delegate: Why are you still here?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>VioletDreamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298977#post1298977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The elemental cycles and the seasons are both described in Third Edition. They're mentioned in the corebook on Page 73. They're not exactly the same things so I don't think people would use them interchangeably, rather they're just two elements of the calendar. And like in real life, there are probably places where the seasons don't break down in quite the same way and people use terms like rainy season versus dry season, monsoon season, etcetera. There's also probably places that don't use this calendar, but you know, it's a game, so for the sake of ease, here's one you can use for your game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I solemnly pledge that from now on I will read the whole sidebar before posting on the forums, not just the first paragraph.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24421-katamari_naneke","name":"Katamari_Naneke","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-23T21:46:03-07:00","id":1299132,"title":"","content":"The stars don't disappear during Calibration. That was fanon that was briefly canonized for a time during 2e, but the sky isn't just a black void anymore.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-24T03:40:19-07:00","id":1299162,"title":"","content":"The only description of what Calibration looks like is in the text of Demon of the Third Circle, and it describes the sky as &quot;starless&quot; (Exalted Third Edition, p. 483).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-24T06:24:59-07:00","id":1299192,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The MG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1299162#post1299162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only description of what Calibration looks like is in the text of Demon of the Third Circle, and it describes the sky as &quot;starless&quot; (Exalted Third Edition, p. 483).</div> </div> </div> </div>If that's &quot;the only description,&quot; you weren't looking very hard. <br /> <br /> Page 73 describes it as when Luna hides her face, without mentioning the stars disappearing; moreover, the passage you're describing is what happens <i>when you summon Ligier</i>. <br /> <br /> I'd expect <i>the emergence of a brilliant light</i> to be sufficient to cause the stars to seem to disappear, as that happens every day at dawn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 03-24-2019, 09:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-24T09:08:57-07:00","id":1299231,"title":"","content":"I'd call that a nonintuitive reading of the passage, but this is also going to be a fruitless argument about nothing,<i> so.</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SamuraiMujuru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation#post1298612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something I've always kinda had a difficulty with is figuring out what exactly the climate of each region is like in Creation, so I was wondering if anyone out there has made something like a climate map, or has some solid guidelines.</div> </div> </div> </div> My stance is that you should look at specific areas and climes and place them where you think they make sense, rather than trying to work out the general climate of these continent-sized regions.<br /> <br /> You want a Florida? It probably goes in the Southwest. Patagonia? The Northwest or the southern coast of the White Sea. Madagascar? I'd say the Dreaming Sea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-24T10:53:00-07:00","id":1299272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The MG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1299231#post1299231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd call that a nonintuitive reading of the passage, but this is also going to be a fruitless argument about nothing,<i> so.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>I think that having a solid idea of what Calibration is supposed to be like is desirable for the setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stephen Lea Sheppard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/835500-3ed-creation-s-calendar?p=835530#post835530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Core is meant to be self-contained. No one would reasonably read &quot;During Calibration there is no visible moon in the night sky&quot; to mean &quot;During Calibration there is no visible moon, <i>nor are there visible stars</i>, in the night sky.&quot; 3e core says during Calibration there's no moon. Doesn't mention stars. A reasonable read of that is, there's no moon but there are stars. It'd be <i>gauche</i> for the 3e core to <i>specifically</i> refute tiny 2e setting details like that.<br /> <br /> (And this is why I always found claims that since all of 1e and the 2e core never <i>specifically</i> said there were stars in the sky during Calibration, it's <i>technically not a retcon</i> to declare there are none to be really grating.)</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Minton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/56091-questions-i-really-want-the-next-corebook-to-answer?p=56303#post56303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Calibration is moonless, as will be restated in the 3e setting chapter. No other unusual celestial phenomena are guaranteed.<br /> <br /> Note that the text will not explicate a negative. It's not the role of the corebook to explicitly point out that the sun <i>does</i> shine and the stars <i>do</i> come out during Calibration, as such things are already the default state of affairs.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 03-24-2019, 02:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-24T22:03:36-07:00","id":1299514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>armyofwhispers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1298612-climate-of-creation?p=1298992#post1298992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solstice and equinox dates are not very well mapped to the true seasons or temperature, though this is probably more evident in some locations than others</div> </div> </div> </div>Picking twelve major cities in the temperate region of the Northern Hemisphere, and the climate data from them (using monthly average high and average low):<br /> <br /> Athens: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Berlin: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> London: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Moscow: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Rome: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> <br /> Beijing: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Seoul: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Tokyo: Warmest months July, August, September; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> <br /> Chicago: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Dallas: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> Los Angeles: Warmest months July, August, September; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> New York: Warmest months June, July, August; coldest months December, January, February.<br /> <br /> So if you wanted to move the official start of the seasons in the Earth calendar to, say, ten days before the solstice/equinox, putting more of December/June in winter/summer and more of March/September in spring/fall, that'd be somewhat more accurate to temperatures. But the convention of starting each season on the solstice/equinox is not particularly far off, especially given that the people who set the convention didn't have thermometers to make it easy to compare the ten days before the winter solstice to the ten days before the spring equinox.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}